<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- averdesign: the Roslyn half of reading an actor script.

       WHY IT IS A SEPARATE PROCESS. The alternative is hosting Roslyn inside the editor, which means
       loading a compiler into a process whose job is to draw frames, and which the editor's own
       collectible load context would then have to keep clear of on every script reload. A console
       tool is invoked, answers, and exits; nothing about the editor's lifetime touches it.

       WHY IT IS THE FALLBACK AND NOT THE PARSER. The built-in scanner in modules/formats needs no
       .NET, no NuGet and no process, and it is sufficient by construction for the locked grammar
       that docs/DESIGNER_REWRITE.md defines. This runs only when the scanner reports Malformed,
       which is precisely the signal that the text has left that grammar, because somebody hand
       edited inside a region the header told them not to. Neither is a degraded mode; they cover
       different files.

       THE ONLY NUGET CONSUMER IN THIS REPO, and deliberately a small one. Microsoft.CodeAnalysis is
       MIT, so it satisfies the permissive-dependency rule, and the package is VENDORED at
       third_party/nuget so this restores with no network. It used to say the restore was offline
       because "the .NET SDK ships it"; that was false. The SDK carries Roslyn as the compiler's own
       DLLs under Roslyn/bincore and its only restorable .nupkg is FSharp.Core, so the package really
       did come from nuget.org the first time anyone built this. Checking it in is what made the
       sentence true. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <AssemblyName>averdesign</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Aver.Design</RootNamespace>
    <RollForward>LatestMinor</RollForward>
    <!-- The tool is invoked once per file that needs it and its answer is cached by the caller, so
         start-up time matters more than steady-state throughput. -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <TieredPGO>false</TieredPGO>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- PINNED, not floating. A parser whose version can move underneath the build is a parser whose
         output can change between two runs of the same source, and the C++ side compares spans byte
         for byte against text it read itself.

         5.6.0 IS THE NEWEST release/stable ROSLYN. Not 5.9.0, which is what this machine's SDK
         actually runs (`csc 5.9.0-1.26379.115`): its nuspec is built from release/insiders and it
         depends on a prerelease Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Analyzers. A parser that reads a user's
         gameplay script should not be the first prerelease in the tree.

         WHY IT MOVED OFF 4.13.0 AT ALL. `csc -langversion:?` on the installed SDK ends
         "... 13.0 14.0 (default)"; Roslyn 4.13 stops at C# 13. That left this tool reading actor
         scripts with a grammar one language version behind the compiler that builds them, and
         Program.cs never inspects diagnostics, so a construct it cannot parse produces a recovered
         tree and a confident answer rather than an error. Roslyn's recovery turns out to be good
         enough that no fixture tried so far actually diverges, so this is a latent hazard closed,
         not a bug fixed; the honest reason to do it is that the version should not trail the
         compiler in the first place. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" Version="5.6.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
